{"id":"73f2c0ed-36f3-4671-a504-0d9cacddf46c","object":"chat.completion","created":1785014800,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\n  \"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    # Validate required fields\\n    required_fields = ['passing', 'failing', 'required_difference', 'unresolved_difference']\\n    for f in required_fields:\\n        if f not in case:\\n            raise ValueError(f\\\"Missing field: {f}\\\")\\n\\n    passing = case['passing']\\n    failing = case['failing']\\n    required_diff = case['required_difference']\\n    unresolved_diff = case['unresolved_difference']\\n\\n    # Build ordered difference (elements in failing but not in passing)\\n    passing_set = set(passing)\\n    difference = [x for x in failing if x not in passing_set]\\n\\n    required_set = set(required_diff)\\n    unresolved_set = set(unresolved_diff)\\n\\n    test_count = 0\\n\\n    def test(subset):\\n        nonlocal test_count\\n        test_count += 1\\n        subset_set = set(subset)\\n        # check unresolved\\n        if subset_set & unresolved_set:\\n            return 'UNRESOLVED'\\n        if required_set.issubset(subset_set):\\n            return 'FAIL'\\n        return 'PASS'\\n\\n    # Initial check: full set must fail\\n    if test(difference) != 'FAIL':\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"Full difference does not cause failure (required_difference not present)\\\")\\n\\n    # ddmin algorithm\\n    def ddmin(c):\\n        # c is list of elements in original order\\n        if test(c) == 'PASS':\\n            return []\\n        return ddmin2(c, 2)\\n\\n    def ddmin2(c, n):\\n        if len(c) == 1:\\n            return c\\n        granularity = n\\n        if granularity > len(c):\\n            granularity = len(c)\\n        # Generate subsets (ordered) covering all elements\\n        subsets = []\\n        for i in range(granularity):\\n            start = i * len(c) // granularity\\n            end = (i + 1) * len(c) // granularity\\n            subsets.append(c[start:end])\\n        # Test each subset\\n        for subset in subsets:\\n            if test(subset) == 'FAIL':\\n                return ddmin2(subset, max(2, granularity - 1))\\n        # Test complements\\n        for subset in subsets:\\n            complement = [x for x in c if x not in subset]\\n            if test(complement) == 'PASS':\\n                # remove subset from c\\n                new_c = [x for x in c if x not in subset]\\n                return ddmin2(new_c, max(2, granularity - 1))\\n        # If no reduction possible, increase granularity\\n        if granularity < len(c):\\n            new_n = min(2 * granularity, len(c))\\n            return ddmin2(c, new_n)\\n        else:\\n            return c\\n\\n    isolated = ddmin(difference)\\n    return {\\n        'isolated_difference': isolated,\\n        'tests': test_count\\n    }\"\n}","reasoning_content":"We need to produce a Python implementation of solve(case) for task SE-DD-02. The task is to isolate a minimal causal difference (difference isolation) between passing and failing test cases. The problem is Delta Debugging (ddmin) to find minimal set of changes that cause failure.\n\nFrom the payload: we have \"passing\" list of elements, \"failing\" list of elements, \"required_difference\" (list?), \"unresolved_difference\". The goal is to find minimal set of changes that are in failing but not in passing, using ddmin to reduce to minimal isolated difference.\n\nThe output must have \"isolated_difference\" (array of strings) and \"tests\" (integer count of tests performed). The schema requires these two keys.\n\nWe must implement ddmin algorithm on the difference set (failing minus passing). The oracle is defined by the invariant: given a subset of changes applied to passing? Actually typical Delta Debugging: we have a set of changes (the difference), we want to find a minimal subset that still causes the failure. The oracle: given a subset of changes, we can test if the failure is present. But we are not given the oracle function; we need to simulate it using the provided fields?\n\nWait the problem says: \"Compute the ordered change set present in failing but absent from passing. Run ddmin only on this difference. Preserve the registered PASS/FAIL/UNRESOLVED oracle semantics.\"\n\nBut we don't have a real oracle. The payload includes \"required_difference\" and \"unresolved_difference\". Perhaps these serve as oracle results? The required_difference is the set of changes that must be present for failure? And unresolved_difference maybe ambiguous? Actually reading the task description: it's about \"difference isolation\" and \"validating every required field before computing a result\". The provided fixtures have required_difference and unresolved_difference. So the oracle is embedded: for a given subset of the difference, we can check if it includes all required_difference and avoids unresolved_difference? Or maybe the oracle is that failure occurs if and only if the subset includes all of required_difference and none of unresolved_difference? But unresolved_difference is empty in these cases.\n\nThat seems plausible: for each subset we can test if it \"covers\" the required difference (i.e., contains all of required_difference). If yes then it's FAIL (failure), else PASS. And unresolved_difference indicates elements that when present may cause indeterminate? But in fixtures it's empty.\n\nSo we need to implement ddmin on the ordered change set (list of strings from difference). The difference is the elements present in failing but not in passing. In the given cases, passing list is [\"c0\",\"c1\",\"c2\"], failing list includes those plus x0..x5, so difference is [\"x0\",\"x1\",\"x2\",\"x3\",\"x4\",\"x5\"] (order as they appear? The failing list order: [\"c0\",\"c1\",\"c2\",\"x0\",\"x1\",\"x2\",\"x3\",\"x4\",\"x5\"]; so difference order is x0,x1,x2,x3,x4,x5).\n\nRequired difference is subset of that. For case 000: required_difference = [\"x4\",\"x5\"]; so minimal failure set includes both x4 and x5. So isolated_difference should be [\"x4\",\"x5\"] and tests count should be the number of tests performed during ddmin.\n\nWe need to implement ddmin algorithm. Typical algorithm:\nInput: set of changes C (list). If testing C fails? Actually we start with full set of changes. We want minimal subset that still fails.\n\nStandard ddmin algorithm (Zeller):\nddmin(C):\n  if test(C) == PASS return [] (no minimal)\n  return ddmin2(C, 2)\n\nddmin2(C, n):\n  if len(C) == 1: return C\n  let testC = test(C)\n  for each of first n subsets (granularity) of size |C|/n:\n    test subset. If test(subset) == FAIL: return ddmin2(subset, max(2, n-1))\n  for each complement:\n    if test(complement) == PASS: return ddmin2(C \\ complement, max(2, n-1))\n  else if n < len(C): return ddmin2(C, min(2*n, len(C)))\n  else: return C\n\nBut we have to count tests. Also we need to preserve order. The output isolated_difference must be in same order as original difference? The schema says isolated_difference is array of strings, but order not specified. However the problem says \"Retain source order through every subset and complement.\" So we should maintain the original order of elements. When computing subsets, they should be in the original order.\n\nWe need to define the test function: given a subset list (preserving order), check if it contains all required_difference (order irrelevant? Required_difference is list, but we can treat as set). If yes, failure (return FAIL). If not, PASS. What about unresolved_difference? It's empty, so ignore. In general, if subset contains any element from unresolved_difference, test is UNRESOLVED? But the output only expects minimal isolated difference and number of tests. The algorithm should handle UNRESOLVED as not causing failure? Typically ddmin should not consider unresolved. The problem says \"Preserve the registered PASS/FAIL/UNRESOLVED oracle semantics.\" So we need to treat UNRESOLVED as a third state. However in these fixtures unresolved is empty, so we can implement generic: check if subset contains any element from required_difference (maybe all required_difference must be present? The typical definition: failure is caused by set of changes that are necessary. In the paper, it's about finding minimal set that reproduces failure. So a subset reproduces failure if it \"covers\" the failure cause. So if required_difference is the set of changes that cause failure, then any superset of that set should also cause failure. But is that guaranteed? In delta debugging, we assume monotonic: if a set causes failure, any superset also causes failure. So we need to check: if required_difference is subset of the test set, then failure. But we don't have \"oracle\" in the payload besides required_difference. So we can assume that.\n\nBut careful: the payload also includes \"unresolved_difference\". In delta debugging, unresolved means we cannot determine failure outcome, perhaps due to interactions. For simplicity, we can treat any test result that includes any unresolved element as UNRESOLVED, and algorithm should treat it as not FAIL (so it doesn't reduce). However the problem states \"Preserve ... oracle semantics.\" So we need to return the number of tests; each call to test function counts as one test.\n\nWe'll implement test(subset) returning string \"PASS\", \"FAIL\", or \"UNRESOLVED\". Then ddmin algorithm uses these results to decide which subsets to recurse.\n\nThe output requires \"tests\" as integer count. So we need a counter.\n\nImplementation steps:\n\n1. Validate case payload: must have 'passing', 'failing', 'required_difference', 'unresolved_difference'. Also they must be lists of strings? We'll assume.\n\n2. Compute difference = elements in failing but not in passing, preserving order (the order they appear in failing). Actually we should maintain the order as they appear in failing? Or as they appear in the set difference? The problem says \"ordered change set present in failing but absent from passing\". So we iterate over failing and include element if not in passing, preserving order. That gives the full difference list.\n\n3. Define set of required_diff_set and unresolved_diff_set.\n\n4. Define test function: given subset (list), check if any element in subset is in unresolved_diff_set? Not sure; typically unresolved means we can't determine. If any unresolved present, result is \"UNRESOLVED\". Else if required_diff_set is subset of set(subset), result \"FAIL\", else \"PASS\". However, does \"required_difference\" mean all of them are needed? The name suggests they are the required differences to cause failure. So if any of required_difference is missing, failure does not occur. So yes.\n\n5. Implement ddmin with test counting. Use recursion or iterative.\n\n6. Return {\"isolated_difference\": result_list (in original order), \"tests\": count}.\n\nWe must not import anything besides standard library; we only need basic types.\n\nPotential edge: The test for full set should be FAIL because full set includes required diff. So ddmin will reduce.\n\nWe need to handle the case where required_difference is empty? Then all subsets are PASS? Actually if required_difference empty, then failure doesn't require any changes? That would be inconsistent; but we can handle.\n\nWe'll implement the classic ddmin algorithm.\n\nLet's write the Python code.\n\nNow, to produce the JSON output with single key \"implementation\". The value is the Python source code as a JSON string with escaped newlines etc.\n\nWe need to ensure it's exactly one JSON object.\n\nLet me write the solution."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":1278,"completion_tokens":2639,"total_tokens":3917,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":896},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":1928},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":896,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":382},"system